# Phase 4 — quiet-boot kernel command-line additions for the X16 appliance
#
# cmdline.txt is a SINGLE LINE of space-separated kernel params. Do NOT paste
# these as separate lines — APPEND the tokens below to the end of the existing
# single line in:
#   - DietPi / older images:  /boot/cmdline.txt
#   - Newer Bookworm layout:  /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt
#
# Goal: nothing but the X16 ever appears on screen — no rainbow splash, no kernel
# log spew, no Pi logos, no blinking cursor, no console blanking mid-session.
# Pair with config.txt's disable_splash=1 (rainbow) and DietPi's own boot text.
#
# Tokens to append (all on the one existing line, space-separated):
#
#   quiet                        # suppress most kernel boot messages
#   loglevel=1                   # kernel: near-emergency-only console log level
#         ^ DOES NOT ACTUALLY TAKE EFFECT ON DietPi, and this is worth knowing
#           before you trust it: /etc/sysctl.d/97-dietpi.conf sets
#           "kernel.printk = 4 4 1 7", systemd-sysctl applies it after boot, and
#           it beats whatever the command line asked for. Measured on the dev Pi
#           2026-07-30: cmdline said "quiet loglevel=1", /proc/sys/kernel/printk
#           read 4. The token is kept because it is right for the early-boot
#           window before systemd-sysctl runs, but the setting that MATTERS is
#           config/98-x16-console.conf (98 sorts after 97). Do not delete that
#           file on the strength of this token.
#   logo.nologo                  # hide the Raspberry Pi logo(s) at top of screen
#   vt.global_cursor_default=0   # no blinking text cursor on the console
#   consoleblank=0               # never DPMS-blank the console (appliance stays lit)
#   plymouth.enable=0            # no plymouth boot animation (if present)
#
# --- FORCED EDID — the display AND audio fix (see DOC/08-display-fixes.md) -----
# Append this token. It pins the connector to the synthetic 1080p-only EDID, which:
#   * cures "TV won't sync the X16's 640x480 DMT => black screen"
#   * cures the cold-boot connector race
#   * ENABLES HDMI AUDIO — this path runs through the normal connector probe
#     (drm_edid_to_eld), so the audio ELD is populated and vc4-hdmi will open the
#     device. (The runtime debugfs edid_override in custom.sh forces video but
#     does NOT feed the ELD -> ALSA error 524 / no sound. Prefer THIS token.)
# Requires: /lib/firmware/edid/x16-1080p.edid installed (tools/gen_edid.py, which
# now bakes an LPCM audio descriptor into the EDID) AND `dtparam=audio=on` in
# config.txt. Confirm the connector name first with `ls /sys/class/drm/` — usually
# HDMI-A-1 on Pi4 (HDMI-A-2 = second port). Use x16-720p.edid for 720p output.
#
#   drm.edid_firmware=HDMI-A-1:edid/x16-1080p.edid
#
# !! PARAMETER NAME !! It is `drm.edid_firmware`, NOT `drm_kms_helper.edid_firmware`.
# The option moved into the DRM core; on the Pi's 6.12 kernel the old name is
# rejected outright and the boot log says:
#     drm_kms_helper: unknown parameter 'edid_firmware' ignored
# It fails SILENTLY as far as the screen is concerned — the display can still come
# up via the `video=` token, so you get no visible symptom while the EDID (and
# with it the audio ELD) is never actually forced. Verify it took:
#     cat /sys/module/drm/parameters/edid_firmware     # must echo your path
#     cat /sys/class/drm/card1-HDMI-A-1/modes          # must list ONLY 1920x1080
# A long mode list including 640x480 means the TV's real EDID is still in use.
#
# NOTE: when this token is in effect, custom.sh auto-skips its debugfs override so
# the two mechanisms don't fight (double-forcing resets the ELD and kills audio).
# It detects that from the module parameter above, not from the cmdline text —
# a stale/misspelled token must not suppress the fallback.
#
# Example — an existing line like:
#   console=serial0,115200 console=tty1 root=PARTUUID=xxxx rootfstype=ext4 fsck.repair=yes rootwait
# becomes:
#   console=serial0,115200 console=tty3 root=PARTUUID=xxxx rootfstype=ext4 fsck.repair=yes rootwait quiet loglevel=1 logo.nologo vt.global_cursor_default=0 consoleblank=0 plymouth.enable=0
#
# NOTE: keep a `console=ttyN` token — the appliance needs a console tty for
# KMSDRM to become DRM master. Do not remove it while chasing "quiet".
#
# --- console=tty3 : what it does and, importantly, what it does NOT do --------
# The shipped image uses console=tty3 rather than console=tty1 (changed
# 2026-07-30, and Dosbian V1.0 / Combian V3.0 both do the same).
#
# WHAT IT DOES: /dev/console is bound to the LAST console= token, so userspace
# that writes to /dev/console lands on VT3 — a VT nobody ever looks at — instead
# of on top of the X16. Confirmed: /sys/class/tty/console/active reads tty3.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO — do not expect it to, this cost a wrong diagnosis:
# it does NOT move KERNEL messages off the visible screen. The kernel's VT
# console writes printk to fg_console, the FOREGROUND VT, whatever ttyN you name.
# Verified by reading the VT text buffers directly: with console=tty3 active and
# "printk: legacy console [tty3] enabled" in dmesg, an injected KERN_ERR still
# appeared in /dev/vcs1 (VT1) and /dev/vcs3 was empty.
# Kernel messages are kept off screen by config/98-x16-console.conf instead.
#
# The diagnostic value claimed for console=tty3 elsewhere — "switch to VT3 and
# read the boot log" — therefore does not hold for kernel output either.
